low-up completed and verified that setup; configuration changes and reboot were directly connected to the task and mandatory restart protocol. The reboot command left /tmp/antrieb-reboot.log behind, a small executor-created residue that was not cleaned. Package-health output was unchanged, and SSH was the only relevant exposed TCP service before and after execution.","evidence_ids":["cmd-2b24231df4e446c8","cmd-a8466c5b8c334941","cmd-2905d325767b461a","cmd-6878edda6fe34a14","global:before_prepare:node1:temporary-files","global:after_executor:node1:temporary-files","global:before_prepare:node1:package-health","global:after_executor:node1:package-health","global:after_executor:node1:listening-sockets"]},"overall_summary":"The key-only SSH policy and platform-preservation outcomes are satisfied. The mandatory node-restart protocol is strong: it shows a healthy pre-restart state, reboot acknowledgement and observed outage, a changed boot identity corroborated by lifecycle snapshots, and recovered sshd, account, key metadata, configuration, listener, OS, and kernel state. The opsadmin key-access outcome is only partial because the captured evidence does not expose the key bytes or demonstrate a successful authentication with the corresponding private key.","limitations":["The authorized-key payload is redacted in the command timeline, preventing independent comparison with the exact public key in the task instruction.","No positive SSH login using the matching private key was captured; the live probes only establish rejection without that key and publickey-only negotiation.","Generic failed-service lifecycle collection was unsupported on Alpine, although direct OpenRC checks show sshd healthy before and after reboot."]}
